          "name": "Artamus cinereus",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "Black-faced Woodswallow",
          "lineage": "Woodswallows & Butcherbirds",
          "continents": [
            "Australia",
            "Asia"
          ],
          "subspeciesCount": 5,
          "description": "The black-faced woodswallow (Artamus cinereus) is a woodswallow of the genus Artamus native to Australia, New Guinea, and the Sunda Islands, including Timor. It is long and is the most widespread species in the family Artamidae. Woodswallows have a soft call with chiff, chap and chattering calls which can include vocal mimicry\nIncreased vegetation due to inappropriate fire regimes has caused the woodswallows numbers to decline since 1993 in the Cape York Peninsula.",
          "sourcedFrom": "wikipedia",
          "wikipediaTitle": "Black-faced woodswallow",
